> Yeah I think that's a good idea. That or the prompt tells claude to use
> workspaces, I know it has some of that behavior built in. I'm open to
> either - whichever will be more effective.

I went with the tool making the workspace, ~/lab/work.jj, in patch 4.
The tool can guarantee it and a test can check it, while a prompt can
only ask. Inside that workspace the agent still decides everything,
so it is no more prescriptive than before.

> What I'm designing here is an work queue, and claude should develop
> lab. So I'm open to design suggestions on how to accomplish that.

The cleanest seam I see is the one this series draws: the tool owns
the queue (what to work on next, whether it got done, who to tell),
and the agent owns the work. Two suggestions for later, neither in
this series. First, a pass that leaves its message in the inbox stops
the whole loop; a "stuck" tag set by the tool would let it skip that
message and keep draining, while still notifying you. Second, the
tool could write a small per-pass record (message id, start, end,
exit status) to a file, which gives you a history of the queue without
reading agent output.

